TERM: "WARM" / "COLD" (capacitor language; defined in the draft, §1.1; use it the same way everywhere)
  "Warm" is the engine's own word and it comes from the capacitor. ValaQuenta/capacitor.py, class Capacitor, is an RC integrator: time constant tau = the conversational window; each sample moves the stored
  charge V toward the signal, V <- V + (signal - V)/tau; it passes the slow component (the word's identity) and smooths away surface variation; transfer function 1/(1 + s*tau). In the monad the stored
  charge enters the word count: VAPMIP/monad.py weighs the stored side (V, beta*E^2, "stored charge, warm field") against the flowing side (T): n_additional = n_words * sumT/(sumT + sumV); the starter always
  fires first (_starter(prompt, warmup=8)) to charge the field before the words are chosen. COLD field = no charge; WARM field = charged.
  In the benchmark (benchmarks/all_voices/benchmark_voices.py): round 1 over the six prompts is COLD (module just loaded, WordNet not yet read in), round 2 is WARM (same six prompts, after round 1 has charged
  the field and filled the caches). Every "warm ms" and warm-energy figure in the paper, README and wiki 42 is round 2. The benchmark does NOT separate the capacitor's charge from the one-time loading inside the cold
  round: report the cold/warm gap as measured, do not attribute it to either cause. (Use engineered-operator wording in the paper: RC integrator, time constant, stored charge; no theory names.)

